2 PEDI step
1 NOTE Arolsen Archives, IRO "Care and Maintenance" personal file 3.2.1.1 / 32110000 163.023, "KOSCHEMBAHR, GEORG, born 6-Oct-1921 München" (DocIDs 79320335-79320342). Filed as "Koschembahr v. Nemirowitsch-Dantschenko, Georg"; Russian, Nansen passport (stateless); Greek Orthodox. Interview of 9 Apr 1948: parents were Russian emigres; he grew up in Berlin; finished the Russian Gymnasium there in 1939, then studied at the Technische Hochschule Berlin (1935-43). His father died in 1939. His mother then married Friedrich Koschembahr, now in the USA, who adopted him. In 1943, as a stateless Russian, he was sent as a labour conscript to Korneuburg near Vienna. A military court sentenced him for sabotage to 5 years' hard imprisonment ("Schwerkerker"), and he was held in a prison in Budapest from May 1944 to Feb 1945. After liberation he went to Munich (May 1945) and studied at the Technische Hochschule München. In Apr 1948 the Bavarian State Ministry certified him as a persecuted person, and the IRO found him eligible as a "persecuted person". Addresses: Rabenkopfstr. 34, München (Apr 1948). Spoke Russian, German and English.
1 NOTE Born Georg von Nemirowitsch-Dantschenko. Frederick W. von Koschembahr's 1941 Declaration of Intention lists him as "von Nemirowitsch-Dantschenko, Georg (stepson)", born 6 Oct 1921 in Munich and then living in Berlin. He later used his stepfather's surname.
1 NOTE Sources: US Census 1950; Social Security NUMIDENT; MA Naturalization Index; Find a Grave Index. Lived in St. Augustine, Florida in 1985.
